✅ 10. Demonstrate WAITING using wait()

10.1 Definition
wait() is a method of the Object class.

It causes the current thread to:
🔴 Release the monitor lock.
🔴 Enter the WAITING state.
🔴 Wait indefinitely until another thread calls notify() or notifyAll() on the same object.

Method Signature

public final void wait() throws InterruptedException

10.2 Why Do We Need wait()?

Imagine a Producer-Consumer problem.

Consumer: Consume Item
But the buffer is empty.
Should the Consumer continuously check?

Is Item Available?
↓
No
↓
Check Again
↓
No
↓
Check Again

This wastes CPU (Busy Waiting).

Instead :

Buffer Empty
↓
Consumer waits()
↓
Producer adds item
↓
Producer notify()
↓
Consumer resumes

This is much more efficient.

10.3 Why is wait() in Object instead of Thread?

This is one of the most asked interview questions.

Incorrect Thinking
🔴 Thread should wait.

Correct Thinking
🔴 Threads don't wait on threads.
🔴 Threads wait on shared objects (monitors).

Example:

synchronized(buffer){
    buffer.wait();
}

The thread is waiting for the buffer object's monitor.
Since every Java object has a monitor, wait() belongs to Object.

10.4 Syntax
synchronized(lock){
    lock.wait();
}

